    "result_text": "The full test suite is green: **35 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**, BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented email-uniqueness rejection for owner creation, following the existing telephone-uniqueness pattern in the codebase.\n\n**New files:**\n- `rest/escalation/DuplicateEmailException.java` \u2014 checked exception carrying the normalized (lower-cased) email.\n- `rest/escalation/DuplicateEmailHandler.java` \u2014 maps the exception to a 409 `ProblemDetail` with `errors: [\"email\"]`.\n- `rest/function/owner/CheckOwnerEmailUnique.java` \u2014 compares the request's lower-cased email against every existing owner's lower-cased email; skips the check when email is absent (email is optional per `NormalizeOwnerEmail`).\n- `officefloor/escalation/\u2026DuplicateEmailException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Modified:**\n- `officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `checkEmailUnique` step right after `normalizeEmail` (so the incoming email is already lower-cased) and before the write steps, under the same `transaction` governance as its neighbors.\n\nThe check runs after normalization so case-insensitive collisions (\"Foo@X.com\" vs \"foo@x.com\") are caught, and a duplicate lower-cased email now returns 409 while unique/absent emails proceed unchanged.",
